        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            All three tools work, and none of them works alone. Use the fastest applicator for the surface — a pad or roller on the deck floor, a sprayer on balusters and lattice, a brush on handrails and end grain — and follow every one of them immediately with a brush.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Back-brushing is the step that decides the outcome, because it is what puts the stain into the wood rather than on it. A sprayed deck that was never brushed looks fine for three weeks and then goes blotchy and tacky, and that single omission causes more deck staining failures than any product choice.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Beyond that, match the bristle to the carrier, load rollers moderately, mask thoroughly and do not spray in a breeze. Work two or three boards at a time from end to end, and if you can find a second pair of hands, use them — one applying and one brushing is the arrangement that makes the whole job easy.
          </p>
        </div>


